  commit afc50add9a0efc82b64fbefcaf6023d9c021e2d1
  Author: Alexandre Laroche <larochealexandre@hotmail.com>
  Date:   Wed Sep 23 21:17:03 2026 -0400
  
      feat(dart): promote to GA with type/import equivalences
      
      Vendors the wrapper-grammar fixes from ocaml-tree-sitter-semgrep#600
      (semgrep_dot_ellipsis_selector, semgrep_statement_list, class-body and
      top-level ellipsis arms) directly into this repo now that
      libs/ocaml-tree-sitter-semgrep is in-tree rather than a submodule, and
      regenerates languages/dart/tree-sitter/semgrep-dart from it.
      
      On top of that, closes the remaining GA gaps for Dart from PR #11735:
      - Typing.ml: && / || are boolean-typed for Dart (spec 17.21).
      - Parse_dart_tree_sitter.ml: populate id_info.id_type on typed locals
        and class fields so Naming_AST propagates types to use sites.
      - Naming_AST.ml / Naming_utils.ml: canonicalize Dart import segments
        (dart_canonical_segments) so patterns written against a library's
        conventional prefix match code that imports it under any alias.
      - Unit_engine.ml: Dart moves to the GA maturity tier.
      
      Also bumps the vendored upstream tree-sitter-dart pin from 02e9bb191
      to be07cf711 (4 commits: Dart 3+ highlighting, labeled statements,
      prec-based operator precedence, and the a.b<c>(d) generic-invocation
      ambiguity), and updates Parse_dart_tree_sitter.ml for the resulting
      CST changes:
      - additive/multiplicative/bitwise-and/bitwise-or/bitwise-xor/shift
        binary expressions switched from a "base + (op, rhs) list" shape to
        a single-op binary node (left-associativity now comes from nesting,
        not a fold), and multiplicative operands widened from
        unary_expression to real_expression.
      - labeled statements are now their own CST node; maps to G.Label.
      One existing parsing snapshot (switch_object_pattern.dart) changed
      because Box<int>(42) was previously misparsed as the relational chain
      (Box < int) > (42); it now parses correctly as a generic constructor
      call, which is what that fix was for.
      
      Includes fixture coverage under tests/patterns/dart, tests/parsing/dart,
      and tests/rules, plus a refresh of parsing snapshots whose other diffs
      were an unrelated id_svalue representation change already on develop.
      
      Ports semgrep/semgrep#11735 (by AlexLaroche) onto the post-sync in-tree
      grammar workflow, per malaverdiere's guidance on that PR: rebase, fold
      in the ocaml-tree-sitter-semgrep#600 companion change, regenerate.
      
      Addresses review on #11928 (dart_canonical_segments false-positive risk):
      two different packages whose main files share a basename, e.g.
      `package:http/http.dart` and `package:other/http.dart`, both naively
      reduced to the `http` prefix, letting a pattern targeting one match the
      other. Naming_utils.unsafe_canonicals is a new, language-agnostic helper
      that groups a file's imports by candidate canonical name and flags any
      name claimed by more than one distinct import; Naming_AST runs it once
      per Dart file and falls back to the unreduced import for any flagged
      name instead of conflating the two. Go and other basename-reducing
      languages hit the same class of collision through a different,
      unguarded code path and could reuse this helper later, but only Dart is
      wired up here. Adds tests/patterns/dart/dart_import_package_collision.*
      (the collision itself) and dart_import_collision_scoped.* (confirms the
      guard is scoped per canonical name, not file-wide).
      
      Addresses review on #11928 (Unit_engine.ml's maturity check only verifies
      fixture existence and successful parsing, not actual behavior): adds
      targeted behavioral tests for six specific claims.
      - metavar_typed_bool.* : `foo($X as bool)` matches `a && b` / `a || b`,
        not an int argument (Typing.ml's Dart boolean-typing change).
      - metavar_typed_field.* / metavar_typed_param.* : a class field's and a
        function parameter's declared type reach a bare-identifier use site
        inside a method/function body, with a differently-typed negative case.
        Both already worked end-to-end; no fix needed.
      - tests/parsing/dart/labeled_statement.dart : confirms `outer: while
        (...) { break outer; }` produces a real `Label` node wrapping the
        `While`/`Break`, not a placeholder.
      - operator_precedence.* : `$X + $Y * $Z` matches `a + b * c` but not
        `(a + b) * c`, exercising the prec-based grammar fix structurally.
      - generic_method_invocation.* : `obj.method<int>(42)` parses and matches
        a call-shaped pattern instead of mis-parsing as the relational chain
        `(obj.method < int) > (42)`. Note: this only proves the call parses
        correctly; the `<int>` type argument itself is not yet threaded into
        the Generic AST's Call node (pre-existing `_tyargs_TODO` in
        map_selector_choice, predating this PR), so a pattern can't yet
        distinguish `method<int>(...)` from `method<String>(...)`. Left as-is
        since it's a separate, pre-existing limitation outside this PR's scope.
      
      Fixes the generic-invocation type-argument drop flagged in review
      (obj.method<int>(arg)): map_selector_choice's Arg_part case discarded
      the type_arguments half of argument_part entirely. Adds
      add_type_arguments_to_callee, threading explicit type arguments onto
      the callee's name_last (method calls) or into TyApply (generic
      constructors), the same convention Rust/Move-on-Sui/Move-on-Aptos use
      for turbofish-style type arguments. generic_method_invocation.sgrep now
      asserts $O.method<int>($X) matches obj.method<int>(42) but not
      obj.method<String>('x') -- previously both a real bug (type args
      silently dropped) and, mid-fix, a compile error from mis-modeling
      map_argument_part's return type as optional when it is not (defaults to
      an empty bracket, not None). Refreshes the switch_object_pattern.dart
      snapshot, whose Box<int>(42) constructor call now correctly carries its
      type argument (TyApply instead of a bare TyN).
      
      Rebased onto develop (now includes #11942, "fix: align generated tree-sitter
      C bindings" -- regeneration picked up the corrected tree_sitter_dart(void)
      signature and (void)unit consumption in bindings.c automatically).
      
      Also fixes the CI grammar-test failure malaverdiere flagged on this PR
      (the semgrep-dart wrapper's inherited copy of tree-sitter-dart's own test
      corpus diverging on "comment selector 1"). Root cause: the wrapper's
      _top_level_definition rule (from ocaml-tree-sitter-semgrep#600, commit
      3b01075) widened what's accepted at the top level of *any* Dart file, not
      just semgrep pattern fragments, so it parses some invalid-at-top-level
      constructs differently than the unmodified base grammar -- which is what
      the inherited test was pinned against. That relaxation was only ever
      needed for multi-statement top-level patterns like `$V = get(); ...;
      eval($V);`, and a later commit (dde5fe3) already handles that properly
      through the isolated __SEMGREP_EXPRESSION entry point via
      semgrep_statement_list, which never touches normal file parsing. Removed
      the now-redundant global relaxation and its associated conflict
      declaration instead of leaving the divergence in place: verified the full
      Dart test suite (including the multi-statement pattern tests) still
      passes end to end through semgrep_statement_list alone, and the
      wrapper's inherited upstream corpus, including "comment selector 1", now
      matches the base grammar exactly with zero exclusions needed.
